Audit: close_live_pool without re-rooting a freshly-allocated result

The gc-repl-handler-case-heap-corruption root cause (v1.8.52) was
read_impl closing its live pool and returning the freshly-read form
UNROOTED — the project convention is

    close_live_pool(pool);
    add_to_live_pool(result);   /* re-root */
    return result;

A mechanical scan (close_live_pool immediately followed by `return
<var>;`) finds ~35 more sites.  Most are SAFE: the returned value is an
existing object reachable from a caller-rooted argument (slot reads,
car/cdr, registry lookups).  The UNSAFE class is a function returning a
FRESHLY ALLOCATED object/graph: any GC between the return and the
caller's first rooting store frees it.  The window is usually a few
instructions — these latent bugs surface as unreproducible one-in-N
corruptions exactly like the REPL one did.

Fixed so far: reader.c read_impl (v1.8.52, the actual crasher),
packages.c make_package, structure.c structure_class_instance_size.

TO AUDIT (classify each: fresh allocation vs reachable-via-argument):
- kernel_structure_functions.c: ~28 sites — GENERATED code
  (sources/generate-structures.lisp): if any are fresh-allocation
  returns, fix the GENERATOR (and regenerate via the patch-file
  mechanism), not the output.
- cons.c bocl_append (single-list early return: safe, returns arg car),
  copy_list (nil case: safe).
- packages.c:175/185 region, and any site the scan pattern misses
  (close followed by an expression return, multiple returns).
- Consider a debug-build assert: a %GC-STRESS=1 run of the full suite
  would catch every unsafe site mechanically — do that once the suite
  runtime under stress is acceptable, or stress per-suite.

RESOLUTION (2026-07-18) — v1.8.58
---------------------------------
Full sweep done.  The mechanical scan (close_live_pool followed by a
return, including expression returns) finds 72 sites; classification:

SAFE — returns a constant or NIL (bocl.c load, eval.c, cons.c reverse
  nil-case): nothing to root.
SAFE — returns a caller argument or a value reachable through one
  (cons.c bocl_append single-list car, copy_list nil, putf existing-key
  plist; environment.c x5 return the environment argument; structure.c
  structure_get / structure_set; packages.c find_external lookup).
SAFE — result stored into a rooted structure before the close
  (kernel.c symbol interning: linked into the obarray; packages.c
  pkg_intern: interned; structure.c instance-slots: memoized into the
  class's cached_slots, the comment even says so).
SAFE — already re-rooted (bytecode.c binds; the v1.8.52/55 fixes).
SAFE — kernel_structure_functions.c, all 48 generated sites: getters
  return a slot value reachable via the caller-rooted instance, setters
  return the caller's own new-value argument.  generate-structures.lisp
  needs NO change.

UNSAFE — fresh object returned unrooted, FIXED (re-root after close):
  1. kernel.c stream_output_string — THE sharpest one: prin1_to_string
     holds the fresh string-stream only in a C local WHILE PRINTING
     INTO IT (which allocates); a GC mid-print could collect the very
     stream being written.  Same family as the v1.8.52 REPL corruption.
  2. kernel.c stream_input_string — fresh input string-stream
     (with-input-from-string's underlying constructor).
  3. callback_stream.c callback stream constructor — fresh stream.

Validation: full 44-suite gate under BOCL_GCSTRESS=8192 (a full GC
every 8192 allocations) — 44/44; reader/eval/clos/strings/conformance/
designators suites under BOCL_GCSTRESS=1024 — 6/6; 100 prin1-to-string
calls under %GC-STRESS 1 (a full GC at EVERY allocation) — clean.
Previously fixed under this pattern: reader.c read_impl (v1.8.52),
packages.c make_package + structure.c instance-size (v1.8.52),
cons.c list()/putf (historical).
